---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PostLifecycle from '../components/PostLifecycle.astro';
import ScopeGate from '../components/ScopeGate.astro';
---

<Base
  title="How it works"
  description="The calls postiee makes, the three traps that break naive LinkedIn clients, and the scope gate that decides what is possible."
>
  <p class="eyebrow">Internals</p>
  <h1>How it works</h1>

  <p class="lead">
    Three details decide whether a LinkedIn CLI works at all. Getting any of
    them wrong produces a tool that appears to succeed and loses data.
  </p>

  <h2>The post lifecycle</h2>

  <figure>
    <PostLifecycle />
    <figcaption>
      Every network call <code>postiee post</code> makes, in order.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Trap one — the ID is in a header</h2>

  <p>
    <code>POST /rest/posts</code> returns <code>201 Created</code> with an
    <strong>empty body</strong>. The post's ID arrives in the
    <code>x-restli-id</code> response header:
  </p>

  <pre><code>HTTP/1.1 201 Created
x-restli-id: urn:li:ugcPost:1234567890123456789

&lt;no body&gt;</code></pre>

  <p>
    A client that reads the response body finds nothing and reports success
    anyway. Because LinkedIn will not let any API read your posts back, that ID
    is the <em>only</em> handle you will ever have on your own post — without it
    you cannot delete it, link to it, or record it.
  </p>

  <p>
    postiee treats a missing header as a hard error that tells you the post may
    already exist, so you check before retrying rather than posting twice.
  </p>

  <h2>Trap two — person IDs are scoped per app</h2>

  <p>
    Every post needs an <code>author</code> field, something like
    <code>{'urn:li:person:8675309'}</code>. That identifier is
    <strong>unique to your application</strong>. The same human has a different
    ID under a different app, and copying one across apps returns 404.
  </p>

  <pre><code>GET https://api.linkedin.com/v2/userinfo
Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN

&#123; "sub": "AbCdEfGh1Jk", "name": "Your Name" &#125;</code></pre>

  <pre><code>author = "urn:li:person:" + sub</code></pre>

  <p>
    This must be resolved at runtime, every time. It cannot be a config value or
    a literal, and it requires the <code>openid</code> scope.
  </p>

  <h2>Trap three — URNs need full percent-encoding</h2>

  <p>
    URNs in URL paths must be completely encoded:
  </p>

  <pre><code>urn:li:ugcPost:12345  →  urn%3Ali%3AugcPost%3A12345</code></pre>

  <p>
    Go's <code>url.PathEscape</code> does <em>not</em> do this — colons are
    legal in a path segment, so it leaves them bare and the request 404s.
    postiee uses <code>QueryEscape</code>, which does encode them. A test pins
    this, because the failure looks like "the post does not exist" rather than
    "you escaped it wrong".
  </p>

  <h2>Two more things worth knowing</h2>

  <h3>Images are two steps, not three</h3>
  <p>
    <code>initializeUpload</code> returns a signed URL <em>and</em> the final
    image URN. You then PUT the bytes and you are done. There is no
    <code>finalizeUpload</code> for images — that step belongs to the Videos API
    and will confuse anyone who assumes otherwise.
  </p>

  <h3>The version header is a moving target</h3>
  <p>
    Every versioned route needs <code>Linkedin-Version: YYYYMM</code>. Versions
    are supported for about twelve months and then sunset. It is pinned to a
    single constant, currently <code>202609</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>The scope gate</h2>

  <p>
    What you can reach depends entirely on which LinkedIn products your developer
    app has been granted — and one of those permissions is closed outright.
  </p>

  <figure>
    <ScopeGate />
    <figcaption>
      Three tiers. postiee operates entirely inside the first.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    This gate is the reason the tool is shaped the way it is. Because
    <code>r_member_social</code> is closed, reading posts back is impossible —
    so the local record becomes the product rather than a feature. Because
    comments and analytics are partner-gated, there is nothing to sync — so the
    CLI stays small enough to be correct.
  </p>

  <h2>Storage</h2>

  <p>
    History lives in a single SQLite file with an FTS5 index over post bodies.
    WAL journalling, one row per post, upsert on the post ID so retrying never
    duplicates.
  </p>

  <pre><code>CREATE TABLE posts (
  post_urn    TEXT PRIMARY KEY,
  body        TEXT NOT NULL,
  permalink   TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT '',
  media_urn   TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT '',
  visibility  TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'PUBLIC',
  created_at  INTEGER NOT NULL
);

CREATE VIRTUAL TABLE posts_fts USING fts5(
  post_urn UNINDEXED,
  body,
  tokenize = 'porter unicode61'
);</code></pre>

  <h2>Search behaviour</h2>

  <p>
    The <code>porter unicode61</code> tokenizer gives stemming, so
    <code>authentic</code> finds <code>authentication</code>. It indexes
    <strong>whole tokens</strong>, so a partial-word prefix does not match:
    <code>search harn</code> will not find <code>harness</code>.
  </p>

  <p>
    That limit is stated plainly rather than hidden behind a workaround, because
    a search that silently returns nothing is worse than one that documents its
    boundary.
  </p>
</Base>